Subject: [PATCH] DeskClock: size the digital widget from its real bounds, and
 stop the framework from dropping the update

Apply in:  <ANDROID_TREE>/packages/apps/DeskClock
Command:   git apply -p1 digital-widget-multidisplay-sizing.patch

PROBLEM
-------
On arcfox (Motorola flip, two internal displays) the DeskClock "Digital clock"
widget on the cover-display home screen has failed in two opposite directions.

(1) With the stock provider the clock was sized for a box far larger than the
    widget and the digits were clipped to slivers.

(2) After the widget was widened to two grid rows the clock collapsed to a
    single pixel.  Measured on device (dumpsys activity com.android.launcher3):

      LauncherAppWidgetHostView  14,137-1030,355  = 1016 x 218 px
        TextClock @id/clock      506,108-510,109  =    4 x   1 px
          TextClock @id/date     0,0-5,1          =    5 x   1 px

    A TextView with includeFontPadding="false" is about 1.16 x textSize tall,
    so a 1 px tall clock means the applied RemoteViews was built with
    mClockFontSizePx == 1 -- the floor optimizeSizes() returns when even a 1 px
    font "overflows" the target box.

ROOT CAUSE
----------
Two independent defects, both required to produce (2).

A. Sizing against an empty options bundle.

   Launcher3 lays out its default workspace through DefaultLayoutParser, which
   binds the widget with the two-argument
   AppWidgetManager.bindAppWidgetIdIfAllowed(appWidgetId, componentName) -- no
   options bundle.  AppWidgetServiceImpl then immediately sends
   ACTION_APPWIDGET_UPDATE while widget.options is still an empty Bundle.
   relayoutWidget() finds no OPTION_APPWIDGET_SIZES, Bundle.getInt() returns 0
   for every MIN_*/MAX_* key, and the clock is laid out for a 0 x 0 box.  Even
   at a 1 px font the sizer still measures at least the layout's own padding
   (widget_padding 16 dp = 36 px per side, so >= 72 px in each dimension), so
   Sizes.hasViolations() is true against 0 x 0 and optimizeSizes() returns its
   1 px floor.  That RemoteViews is what the widget still displays.

   The stock code has exactly the same hole: it reads the same bundle with the
   same Bundle.getInt() and would also size the clock for a 0 x 0 box.

B. Every later, correctly sized update was silently dropped.

   AppWidgetManager.tryAdapterConversion() (frameworks/base) routes any update
   whose RemoteViews contains a legacy collection adapter through
   shouldSkipListConversion():

       return appWidgetIds.length == 1
               && views.hasLegacyLists()
               && mService.isFirstConfigActivityPending(mPackageName, ids[0]);

   and when that is true it strips the intents and *returns without ever
   invoking the update action* -- updateAppWidgetIds() is never called.  It
   logs only "Skipping legacy list conversion, pending config activity".

   digital_appwidget.xml declares android:configure=... without
   android:widgetFeatures="configuration_optional", so
   Widget.isFirstConfigActivityPending is set true at bind time
   (AppWidgetServiceImpl line ~1720) and is never cleared, because a widget
   placed from the default workspace never runs its configuration activity.

   hasLegacyLists() becomes true as soon as any of the per-size RemoteViews
   calls setRemoteAdapter() for @id/world_city_list, which relayoutWidget()
   does when getListHeight() exceeds widget_min_world_city_list_size (80 dp =
   180 px at this density).  At one grid row the tallest advertised size was
   1016 x 417 px, which after the 332 px the widget measures at the maximum
   font leaves 85 px -- below 180 px, so no entry enabled the list, no update
   was ever routed through tryAdapterConversion(), and the widget looked
   correct.  At two grid rows the *inner display's* portrait profile
   contributes 1016 x 862 px and 1016 x 852 px entries; 862 - 332 = 530 px is
   well above 180 px, the list is enabled, hasLegacyLists() flips to true, and
   from that moment on every single update for this widget is discarded.

   Measured, 10:07:05 on device, one relayoutWidget() call:

     V/DigitalWidgetProvider: Target dimensions: 1016px x 218px / font 101px
     V/DigitalWidgetProvider: Target dimensions: 1016px x 862px / font 180px
     V/DigitalWidgetProvider: Target dimensions: 1016px x 852px / font 180px
     V/DigitalWidgetProvider: Target dimensions:  604px x 342px / font 180px
     V/DigitalWidgetProvider: Target dimensions: 1016px x 208px / font  95px
     V/DigitalWidgetProvider: Target dimensions: 2212px x 342px / font 180px
     D/AppWidgetManager: Skipping legacy list conversion, pending config activity
     D/AppWidgetManager: Skipping collection notify, pending config activity

   The provider computed a correct 101 px font for the real 1016 x 218 px
   bounds; the framework threw the update away, and the view stayed 4 x 1 px.

FIX
---
1. res/xml/digital_appwidget.xml: add
   android:widgetFeatures="configuration_optional".
   DigitalAppWidgetConfigurationActivity only chooses a transparent or solid
   background and the widget renders correctly with the default, so
   configuration genuinely is optional.  This clears
   isFirstConfigActivityPending at bind time, so shouldSkipListConversion()
   is false and updates carrying the world-city adapter are delivered.

2. DigitalAppWidgetProvider: never size the clock from anything but bounds the
   host actually reported.

   - OPTION_APPWIDGET_MAX_WIDTH / MAX_HEIGHT are no longer read anywhere.  On a
     device with more than one internal display Launcher3's WidgetSizeHandler
     aggregates them over InvariantDeviceProfile.supportedProfiles, so
     MAX_HEIGHT can describe a display the widget is not being drawn on.  That
     was failure (1).
   - The per-size map from OPTION_APPWIDGET_SIZES is kept: it is the API that
     lets AppWidgetHostView.onLayout() pick the layout matching its real bounds.
   - A synthetic entry is always added first, holding the component-wise
     minimum of OPTION_APPWIDGET_MIN_WIDTH/MIN_HEIGHT and every advertised
     size.  By construction it has the smallest area in the map, so
     RemoteViews.findSmallestRemoteView() -- the layout the host applies before
     it has ever laid the widget out, when mCurrentSize is still null -- is now
     one that fits inside every size the host may choose instead of whichever
     profile happened to have the smallest area.
   - When the options bundle carries no size at all, that synthetic entry falls
     back to AppWidgetProviderInfo.minWidth/minHeight (the appwidget-provider
     XML declaration, 202 dp x 80 dp) and then to a 40 dp floor, so the target
     box can never be 0 x 0.  That was failure (2)A.

On arcfox the host now selects the 1016 x 218 px entry, whose measured clock
font is 101 px.

This is an upstream AOSP/LineageOS bug, not an arcfox port defect: it fires on
any device whose launcher reports more than one device profile with materially
different cell heights (foldables, flip phones, tablets with a cover screen).

NOTE (not fixed here): shouldSkipListConversion() dropping the update instead
of posting the stripped RemoteViews looks like an AOSP bug in
frameworks/base/core/java/android/appwidget/AppWidgetManager.java --
the "skip" branch calls viewsCopy.replaceAllIntentsWithEmptyList() and then
falls out of the if without calling the action.  Making configuration optional
side-steps it; it is worth reporting upstream.

